      "math_academy_id": "q-165246",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6t,\\,{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k).$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =3⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =3\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{4+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $({t}^{2}+2)>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =18\\sqrt{{(-{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =18({t}^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18({t}^{2}+2)}{{(3({t}^{2}+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
